<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="1" to="1" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="0.2" to="0.5" step="0.1"/>

  <function name="f">sqrt(x)</function>
  <derivative name="fP">$f</derivative>
  <derivative name="fPP">$fP</derivative>
  <derivative name="fPPP">$fPP</derivative>
  <derivative name="fP4">$fPPP</derivative>

  <math name="c0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="c1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="c2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
  <math name="c3">$$fPPP($a)/3!</math>
  <math name="c4">$$fP4($a)/4!</math>

  <math simplify name="c">$a + $k</math>

  <function name="T3">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3</function>

</setup>

<p>
  Consider the function <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m>, suppose we want to use Taylor polynomials to approximate the value of <m>\sqrt{x}</m> near <m>a = $a</m> and analyze the error involved in doing so.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
         Find and state the degree <m>3</m> Taylor polynomial of <m>f</m>  centered at <m>a = $a</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="T3Entered" symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare><label><m>T_3(x) = </m></label>$T3</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use <m>T_3(x)</m> to estimate the value of <m>f($c) = \sqrt{$c}</m>.  Report your result using at least <m>6</m> places after the decimal point, if applicable.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="fEst" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>\sqrt{$c} = f($c) \approx T_3($c) = </m></label>$$T3($c)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Finally, we use the Lagrange Error Bound to quantify the error in our approximation in part (b).  Fill in the blanks below appropriately; report any decimal values to an accuracy of at least <m>0.000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that we used the degree <m>3</m> Taylor polynomial of <m>f</m> centered at <m>a=$a</m> to approximate <m>f($c)</m>, which means that in the Lagrange Error Bound formula
      </p>
      <div sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>n + 1 = </m></label>4</answer> and
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>c-a = </m></label>$k</answer>.
      </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Note also that the fourth derivative of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>f^{(4)}(x) = -\frac{15}{16(\sqrt{x})^7}</m>, and on the interval <m>[$a, $c]</m>, the absolute maximum value of <m>|f^{(4)}(x)| = \left|\frac{15}{16(\sqrt{x})^7}\right|</m> is
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>M = </m></label>abs($$fP4($a))</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Using the values of <m>M</m>, <m>|c - a|</m>, and <m>(n+1)</m> in the Lagrange Error Bound formula, it follows that
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>|f($c) - T_3($c)| \le </m></label>abs($$fP4($a))*$k^4/4!</answer>
      </p>

    </li>
  </ol>
</p>